## Why This Is Asked

Duplicate values are common in real-world data sets. Traditional 2-way quicksort performs poorly with many duplicates, leading to unnecessary comparisons and degraded performance. 3-way partitioning addresses this by grouping equal elements together, significantly reducing the algorithm's work.

## Key Concepts

- **3-way partitioning (Dutch national flag algorithm)**: Separates array into < pivot, == pivot, and > pivot regions
- **Randomized pivot selection**: Prevents worst-case O(n²) scenarios on already sorted or adversarial input
- **In-place sorting**: Operates with O(1) additional space, using only O(log n) expected stack depth
- **Performance optimization**: Reduces comparisons from O(n log n) to O(n) when many duplicates exist

## Code Example

```javascript
function quicksort3(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;

  // Random pivot selection
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random() * (hi - lo + 1)) + lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;

  // 3-way partitioning
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) {
      [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]];
      lt++;
      i++;
    } else if (a[i] > pivot) {
      [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]];
      gt--;
    } else {
      i++; // a[i] === pivot
    }
  }

  // Recursively sort subarrays
  quicksort3(a, lo, lt - 1);
  quicksort3(a, gt + 1, hi);
}
```

## Partitioning Strategy

The algorithm maintains three regions during partitioning:
- `[lo, lt-1]`: elements < pivot
- `[lt, gt]`: elements === pivot (being processed)
- `[gt+1, hi]`: elements > pivot

As we iterate, elements are swapped into their appropriate region, with equal elements naturally forming the middle section that needs no further processing.

Why This Is Asked
- Tests dynamic augmentation of a trie with aggregated metrics and tie-breaking logic.

Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with subtreeSum and subtreeBest; O(length) updates; O(length) queries.
- Correct handling of weight deltas, insertions, and removals; lexicographic tiebreaks.

Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.subtreeSum = 0;
    this.bestWord = null;
    this.bestWeight = 0;
  }
}
class DynamicDict {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.wordWeights = new Map();
  }
  add(word, delta) {
    let node = this.root;
    const newWeight = (this.wordWeights.get(word) || 0) + delta;
    this.wordWeights.set(word, newWeight);
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.subtreeSum += delta;
      if (newWeight > node.bestWeight || 
          (newWeight === node.bestWeight && word < node.bestWord)) {
        node.bestWord = word;
        node.bestWeight = newWeight;
      }
    }
  }
  query(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        return [0, null];
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    return [node.subtreeSum, node.bestWord];
  }
}
```

Complexity Analysis
- `add(word, delta)`: O(|word|) time, O(1) extra space per character
- `query(prefix)`: O(|prefix|) time, O(1) extra space

Edge Cases
- Negative deltas potentially removing words completely
- Multiple words with equal maximum weights requiring lexicographic tie-breaking
- Empty queries and non-existent prefixes
- Words sharing common prefixes affecting aggregate calculations

## Why This Is Asked
This problem validates DP mastery (two implementations) and handling of substring lookups with a dictionary. It stresses practical constraints and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Bottom-up DP, Memoization, Time: O(N * maxLen)
- Hash-map lookups for dictionary scores
- Edge cases: no decomposition, empty input, overlapping choices

## Code Example
```javascript
// Bottom-up
function maxScore(s, dict) {
  const maxLen = Math.max(...Object.keys(dict).map(k => k.length));
  const n = s.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(NEG);
  dp[0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= Math.min(i, maxLen); len++) {
      const substr = s.slice(i - len, i);
      if (dict.hasOwnProperty(substr) && dp[i - len] !== NEG) {
        dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[i - len] + dict[substr]);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n] === NEG ? -1 : dp[n];
}

// Top-down
function maxScoreTopDown(s, dict) {
  const maxLen = Math.max(...Object.keys(dict).map(k => k.length));
  const memo = new Map();
  function solve(i) {
    if (i === s.length) return 0;
    if (memo.has(i)) return memo.get(i);
    let best = -Infinity;
    for (let len = 1; len <= Math.min(maxLen, s.length - i); len++) {
      const substr = s.substring(i, i + len);
      if (dict.hasOwnProperty(substr)) {
        const val = dict[substr] + solve(i + len);
        if (val > best) best = val;
      }
    }
    memo.set(i, best);
    return best;
  }
  const res = solve(0);
  return res === -Infinity ? -1 : res;
}
``` 

