## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design streaming state with sliding windows, using hash maps, per-id deques (linked lists), and a heap for ranking. Emphasizes correctness under out-of-order arrivals and lazy expirations.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding-window maintenance per key using a deque
- Lazy expiration and lazy heap invalidation
- Top-K extraction via a max-heap with versioning

## Code Example
```javascript
class RealTimeTopKWindow {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.byId = new Map(); // id -> { deque: [], sum, version }
    this.globalMax = new MinHeap((a,b)=>b.sum-a.sum); // max-heap emulation
  }
  addEvent(id, value, ts) {
    // Ensure id exists
    const rec = this.byId.get(id) || { deque: [], sum: 0, version: 0 };
    // expire old
    while (rec.deque.length && rec.deque[0].ts <= ts - this.W) {
      const old = rec.deque.shift();
      rec.sum -= old.value;
    }
    rec.deque.push({ ts, value });
    rec.sum += value;
    rec.version++;
    this.byId.set(id, rec);
    this.globalMax.push({ sum: rec.sum, id, ver: rec.version });
  }
  getTopK(K) {
    const res = [];
    const seen = new Set();
    while (res.length < K) {
      const top = this.globalMax.pop();
      if (!top) break;
      const cur = this.byId.get(top.id);
      if (!cur || cur.version !== top.ver || top.sum !== cur.sum) continue; // stale
      if (!seen.has(top.id)) {
        res.push(top.id);
        seen.add(top.id);
      }
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if W varies by id? 
- How to scale to millions of ids with bounded memory?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to fuse graph shortest-path ideas with node activation delays and cycle handling, plus choosing between DAG DP and general graph Dijkstra.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra with transformed edge costs w(u,v)+d(v)
- Topological sort for DAG optimization
- Handling multiple sources S
- Non-negative weights guarantee correctness

## Code Example
```javascript
function earliestFinish(V, adj, S, d) {
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = new Array(V).fill(INF);
  const pq = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a[0]-b[0]);
  for (const s of S) { dist[s] = d[s]; pq.push([dist[s], s]); }
  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const [du, u] = pq.pop();
    if (du !== dist[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      const cand = dist[u] + w + d[v];
      if (cand < dist[v]) { dist[v] = cand; pq.push([cand, v]); }
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests dynamic programming skills on a directed acyclic graph (DAG) with multiple predecessor choices and path counting for optimal solutions. The ability to handle several predecessors, implement proper tie-breaking for path counting, and correctly apply modulo arithmetic demonstrates code clarity and robustness with edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- DP on DAG with 3-step transitions (i → i+1, i+2, i+3)
- Maintaining both the optimal value and the count of optimal paths
- Modulo arithmetic (1e9+7) for path counting
- Edge-case handling for small n and negative profit values

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitAndPaths(profits) {
  const n = profits.length;
  const MOD = 1000000007n;
  if (n === 0) return { best: 0, cnt: 1 };
  if (n === 1) return { best: profits[0], cnt: 1 };

  const best = new Array(n).fill(0);
  const cnt = new Array(n).fill(0);
  best[0] = profits[0];
  cnt[0] = 1;

  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    let maxVal = -Infinity;
    for (const p of [i-1, i-2, i-3]) {
      if (p >= 0) {
        const cand = best[p] + profits[i];
        if (cand > maxVal) {
          maxVal = cand;
          cnt[i] = cnt[p];
        } else if (cand === maxVal) {
          cnt[i] = (cnt[i] + cnt[p]) % MOD;
        }
      }
    }
    best[i] = maxVal;
  }
  return { best: best[n-1], cnt: cnt[n-1] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to convert dependency graphs into scheduling via topological order; checks understanding of longest-path DP on DAG, cycle detection, and clear complexity analysis.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort (Kahn / DFS)
- DAG longest path DP
- Cycle detection
- Complexity analysis

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestFinishTimes(n, edges, durations) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  const finish = durations.slice();
  let seen = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    seen++;
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      finish[v] = Math.max(finish[v], finish[u] + durations[v]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  if (seen !== n) throw new Error('Cycle detected');
  return finish;
}
```

